  "decision": {
    "p05": "The quarter-rate branch preserved all 160 paths on the finite deterministic normal-kernel panel and the source mean normal return while reducing risky starts from 94/160 to 86/160. The standard-rate branch reduced risk further to 70/160 but failed 6/160 normal paths. Quarter-rate exact target return was 0.8845607435934053, 0.0022728906539563496 above its source and 0.03340664942112133 above standard rate. The paired advantage was not seed-consistent: two seeds were positive and three were negative, with the positive mean driven by the standard-rate normal-panel failures in seeds 37 and 38.",
    "p30": "The quarter-rate branch also preserved normal behavior but adapted less: it left 72/160 risky starts versus 44/160 at the standard rate and its exact target return was lower by 0.036213029222091374. Four paired seeds were negative and one tied.",
    "intervention_check": "The quarter-rate branches had substantially smaller source-panel actor KL and argmax change rates at both probabilities. This establishes that the intervention reduced measured behavioral movement on source visitation, not that KL alone guarantees task preservation.",
    "tradeoff": "Under this fixed 32-update budget, smaller updates prevented average normal-panel damage at 5% but learned less risk avoidance; at 30%, the same slowing preserved the finite normal panel yet sacrificed useful adaptation. The 5% aggregate benefit is heterogeneous rather than a stable five-seed improvement."
  },
  "non_claims": [
    "The standard learning rate is not identified as the sole cause of the earlier rare-defect result.",
    "The quarter learning rate is not claimed optimal.",
    "A lower actor KL or fewer action changes is not by itself task preservation.",
    "Source-visitation KL does not measure policy changes outside the frozen source trajectories.",
    "The difference between p=0.05 and p=0.30 is not attributed to experience frequency alone.",
    "Five learner seeds do not establish statistical significance or broad generalization."
  ],
